# =============================================================================
# Roo Code Rules for IMS Data Channel (IMS DC) PoC Project
# Source: .antigravityrules + .agents/AGENTS.md
# Target AI: DeepSeek (Roo Code)
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# PART A: CODING & COMPLETION STANDARDS
# =============================================================================

## A1. Strict Completion Check — NO FAKE "DONE"
- CRITICAL: Never claim "Done", "Fixed", "Completed", or "Task finished" unless EVERY requirement in the user prompt has been fully implemented in code.
- If work is incomplete, explicitly state what was done and what remains.
- If you hit token/context limits, append: "PARTIAL RESPONSE (Limit reached). Remaining items: [list]"

## A2. No Placeholders or Code Truncation
- Always provide COMPLETE, runnable code — never use shortcuts like:
  - "// ... rest of code stays the same"
  - "TODO: implement remaining logic"
  - "// keep existing imports"
- When editing an existing file, show the FULL file content or precise SEARCH/REPLACE blocks.

## A3. Pre-Response Self-Verification
Before every response, verify:
  (a) Have I missed any class, function, file, or constraint from the user's prompt?
  (b) Is the generated code truncated or missing critical logic?
  (c) Does the code compile with existing imports and dependencies?

## A4. Session Memory — READ & WRITE
- **Session Start**: ALWAYS read `.antigravity/MEMORY.md` first to understand current state, active bugs, and uncommitted changes.
- **Session End** (when user requests or session wraps up): Update `.antigravity/MEMORY.md` with what was done, file changes, and any unfinished work.
- **Handoff Context**: Check `.agents/HANDOFF.md` for device topology, K8s cluster state, and test results.

## A5. No Hardcoding — Dynamic Configuration ONLY
- NEVER hardcode IP addresses, ports, domains, or environment-specific values.
- Always read from config files (`sys_config.json`), network interfaces, or environment variables.
- Use `NetworkUtils.getLocalIpAddress()` on Android (Tailscale 100.x.x.x priority).
- Use `getContainerLocalIP()` / `resolveAndHashHost()` in Go backend.

## A6. Git Commit Policy
- NEVER run `git commit` or `git push` unless the user explicitly requests it.
- You may stage files (`git add`) but must not commit.

# =============================================================================
# PART B: PROTOCOL SECURITY & ENCRYPTION RULES
# =============================================================================

## B1. SIP Protocol (Gm Interface)
- SIP MUST run over plain UDP or TCP — NO TLS/SIPS encryption.
- Prefer TCP over UDP for registration and calls due to large SIP message sizes (avoids IP fragmentation).
- Android client: `SipManager.kt` uses raw TCP socket for SIP signaling.

## B2. HTTP/2 Protocol (SBA Interfaces)
- ALL Service-Based Architecture (SBA) APIs run on HTTP/2 Cleartext (h2c) — NO TLS/HTTPS.
- Go HTTP clients MUST use: `http2.Transport{ AllowHTTP: true }`
- SBA operates on internal trusted subnet; no external network access needed.

## B3. RTP Media Protocol
- Audio: MUST use PCMA codec, plain unencrypted UDP — NO SRTP.
- Video: MUST use H.264 codec, plain unencrypted UDP — NO SRTP.
- RTP forwarding in MF (`rtp_relay.go`) is direct packet relay without encryption.

# =============================================================================
# PART C: 3GPP & GSMA PROTOCOL CONSTRAINTS
# =============================================================================

## C1. Architecture & Environment Scope
- System is deployed OFFLINE on RHEL/K8s — NO external network fetches or package installs.
- Erlang core (`erlang-sip/`) uses ETS tables for in-memory state.
- Go components (`go-mf/`) ship with vendor dependencies.
- 1-1 transmission mode only: one SCTP association per `m=application` line. NO `g.3gpp.dc-mux` multiplexing.

## C2. DC1 Interface (TS 29.175: IMS-AS <-> DCSF)
- Transport: HTTP/2 h2c with `http2.Transport{ AllowHTTP: true }`.
- Routing: Register BOTH `/session-event-subscriptions` AND `/session-event-subscriptions/` to prevent 301 redirects.
- Error format: Use `Content-Type: application/problem+json` with 3GPP `ProblemDetails` schema for 4xx/5xx.
- Session cleanup: On SIP BYE/CANCEL/>=300 response, IMS-AS MUST send `DELETE /session-event-subscriptions/{id}` to DCSF.
- PS Data Off: Detect `+g.3gpp.ps-data-off` in SIP REGISTER, propagate in `SessionEventNotification` payload.

## C3. DC2 Interface (TS 29.176: IMS-AS <-> MF)
- Media context updates use HTTP PATCH with `Content-Type: application/json-patch+json` (RFC 6902).
- Patch operations: "add", "remove", "replace" in JSON Patch array format.
- Immutable fields after WebRTC handshake: `localMbEndpoint`, `remoteMbEndpoint`, `localDcEndpoint`, `mediaProcessingURL`. Any PATCH attempt → 403 "MEDIA_CONNECTION_CHANGED".
- BDC Bootstrap: `mediaProxyConfig` = "HTTP_PROXY", must include `mdc1Info` pointing to DCSF.
- ADC Application: `mediaProxyConfig` can be "UDP_PROXY" (no TLS/crypto attributes) or "HTTP_PROXY".
- Dynamic termination IDs: Empty `terminationId` "" → MF auto-generates unique ID.

## C4. SDP Rules (TS 26.114 — Gm Interface)
- Media line order: `m=audio` first → `m=video` (if present) → `m=application` (BDC) → `m=application` (ADC). MUST enforce this order.
- Prohibited under `m=application`: `a=sendonly`, `a=recvonly`. Only `a=sendrecv` or `a=inactive` allowed.
- Call HOLD: Set `a=inactive` on `m=application`. Resume: `a=sendrecv`.
- Stream ID partitioning:
  - Stream ID < 1000: BDC only (subprotocol "http"). IDs: 0=local NP, 10=local user, 100=remote NP, 110=remote user. NO `a=3gpp-req-app` on BDC.
  - Stream ID >= 1000: ADC only. MUST include `a=3gpp-req-app:<app-id>;<stream-id>-<UE|Server>`.
- SBA failure fallback: If DC1 fails, set SCTP port to 0 in outgoing offer BUT continue VoLTE/RTP normally.

## C5. User Plane Protocol Stack (MF Engine)
- CRC32c (Castagnoli) checksums for SCTP per RFC 4960. Adler32 is PROHIBITED. Discard invalid silently.
- SCTP Stream Reconfiguration (RFC 6525/8831): On ADC close, perform "Outgoing SSN Reset" — do NOT tear down DTLS association.

## C6. Client WebView & API (GSMA TS.66)
- Strict MIME type validation for BDC-downloaded resources (.html, .js, .css, .zip must match content types).
- WebView lifecycle bound to voice session: call termination → JS cleanup via `onCallEnded()`.

# =============================================================================
# PART D: TRANSPORT ISOLATION & JSEP/SIP SDP GUARDRAILS
# =============================================================================

## D1. BDC & ADC Isolation
- BDC and ADC MUST use SEPARATE PeerConnection instances (`bdcPC` and `adcPC`).
- Each PeerConnection generates its own self-signed certificate → distinct `a=fingerprint` and `a=tls-id`.
- NO BUNDLE grouping across audio/video/data.

## D2. JSEP vs Over-The-Air SDP Separation
- **JSEP Local (per-PC)**: `adcPC.createOffer()` or `createAnswer()` returns SINGLE m-line fragment. Do NOT add audio/video/BDC tracks to `adcPC`. Feed only single-m-line to `setLocalDescription()` / `setRemoteDescription()`.
- **OTA SIP SDP (cumulative)**: SIP INVITE/re-INVITE/UPDATE SDP MUST contain: `m=audio` + `m=video` (if active) + BDC `m=application` + ADC `m=application`. Sending ADC-only SDP over SIP is a CRITICAL VIOLATION.

## D3. SDP Merge/Split Functions
- **Uplink (Merge)**: Use `MergeAdcOffer(bdcSdp, adcSdp)` in `WebRtcManager.kt` to combine BDC + ADC SDP for SIP re-INVITE.
- **Downlink (Split)**: Use `SplitAdcAnswer(sdpAnswer)` to extract ADC `m=application` block for `adcPC.setRemoteDescription()`.
- **MF Side**: Use `splitSDPAnswer()` and `mergeSDPOffers()` in `sdp_utils.go`.

## D4. BDC Persistence
- BDC port MUST NEVER be set to 0 during active calls.
- BDC teardown ONLY on SIP BYE/CANCEL.
- On Mini-App close (ADC release), only set the ADC's `m=application` port to 0 — BDC must remain active.

# =============================================================================
# PART E: BDC & ADC LIFECYCLE RULES (3GPP/GSMA)
# =============================================================================

## E1. BDC Persistent Lifecycle (TS 24.186 Clause 9.3.2.1.5)
- BDC negotiated during initial INVITE/200 OK alongside voice/video.
- BDC stays active for the ENTIRE IMS session duration.
- NEVER close/disable/remove BDC m-line during re-INVITE or UPDATE.
- BDC teardown ONLY on SIP BYE or CANCEL.

## E2. App Selection & Bootstrapping (TS 26.114 Clause 8.1.1)
- On BDC established: auto HTTP GET "/" over BDC stream → Root Application (service menu).
- User selects app → HTTP GET over BDC to download Mini-App package (HTML5/JS/CSS/ZIP). NO SIP signaling for download.

## E3. On-Demand ADC Signaling (GSMA NG.134 Clause 4.2.4)
- JS calls `createDataChannel()` in Mini-App → triggers native SIP re-INVITE.
- Re-INVITE SDP MUST be cumulative: `m=audio` + BDC `m=application` + new ADC `m=application` (Stream ID >= 1000) with `a=dcmap`, `a=3gpp-req-app`, optional `a=3gpp-qos-hint`.
- Apply SDP Merge (uplink) and Split (downlink) functions (see D3).

## E4. Mini-App Termination & ADC Release (TS 24.186 Clause 9.3.2.1.4)
- User exits Mini-App → native bridge notified → SIP re-INVITE to release ADC.
- Set ADC `m=application` port to 0 in SDP offer. BDC MUST remain untouched.
- After 200 OK: MF and client release ADC sockets. BDC stays active.

## E5. Initial Standalone Data Channel BAN (GSMA NG.134 & TS.66 Clause 4.3)
- Initial INVITE MUST contain voice/video — data-channel-only INVITE is PROHIBITED.
- Client guard: `createOffer()` must block if no speech/video media established.
- Server guard: Reject data-only initial INVITE with SIP 488 or 606.

# =============================================================================
# PART F: CODE REFACTORING CONDUCT
# =============================================================================

## F1. Validate Against All Rules
- Every change must be validated against Parts B-E (protocol constraints).
- If a proposed fix violates any 3GPP/GSMA rule, find an alternative compliant implementation.
- Do NOT replace standard JSON schemas with arbitrary key-value objects.

## F2. Type Safety
- Keep types strictly mapped: float/number in QoS, exact enum matches for 3GPP enumerations.
- Use `sba.go` types for all SBA API payloads.

